EXT. LIFEBOAT ON OPEN SEA - DAY

A steel-grey sea under a flat white sky. The boat is a sealed orange cabin with a hatch left open. Four survivors sit shoulder to shoulder on the benches.

RUTH, sixties, a retired purser, wears a stained uniform jacket and holds the emergency kit in her lap. GUSTAVO, thirties, a deckhand, has a gash across one palm wrapped in a sock. ANNELIESE, fifties, a passenger in a torn evening dress, rocks a little, eyes closed. DWAYNE, twenties, the youngest, stares at the hatch, jaw clenched.

Nobody has spoken for a long time.

RUTH
All right. Listen up, everybody. Rations. One cracker each at noon, one at dusk. Water twice a day. I'll mark the bottle.

GUSTAVO
Who made you captain?

RUTH
I'm the one holding the kit.

DWAYNE
(muttering)
Doesn't make it fair.

RUTH
Say it louder, young man.

DWAYNE
I said nothing.

ANNELIESE
(eyes still closed)
Please don't fight. It makes the boat feel smaller.

GUSTAVO
The boat is small.

ANNELIESE
I know it is.

RUTH
(to Gustavo)
Show me the hand.

He holds it out. She unwraps the sock, inspects the cut, wrinkles her nose.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Clean it with salt water. Then wrap it again.

GUSTAVO
Salt will sting.

RUTH
It will.

She hands him the bandage roll. He takes it and does as told, hissing.

DWAYNE
(to himself)
Three days. They said three days.

GUSTAVO
Who said?

DWAYNE
The radio, before it went dead. Three days till a ship.

RUTH
Then we have two more.

DWAYNE
If they're coming.

ANNELIESE
They are coming. I dreamed it.

DWAYNE
Oh, great. A dream.

RUTH
Dwayne.

DWAYNE
Sorry. Sorry.

Ruth opens the kit and counts crackers into four small piles. She hands them out. Gustavo eats his in one bite. Anneliese holds hers in her palm without tasting. Dwayne breaks his in half, saves one half in his pocket.

GUSTAVO
Smart.

DWAYNE
Not smart. Scared.

RUTH
Same thing, out here.

The sea slaps the hull. The boat lifts and settles.

ANNELIESE
(opening her eyes)
Does anyone know a song?

Nobody answers. Then Gustavo, quietly, hums three notes. Dwayne picks it up. Ruth, to her own surprise, joins in. They hum it, thin and cracked, over the empty water.
